WEBVTT

01:01.228 --> 01:03.355
<i>Eye movement detected.</i>

01:18.870 --> 01:20.872
<i>What is two plus two?</i>

01:22.708 --> 01:24.293
<i>Incorrect.</i>

01:25.002 --> 01:26.378
<i>Cognition assessment...</i>

01:27.671 --> 01:28.880
<i>What is two plus two?</i>

01:29.673 --> 01:31.008
<i>Incorrect.</i>

01:31.675 --> 01:34.261
<i>You have been in an induced coma.</i>

01:36.471 --> 01:38.432
<i>You may experience</i>
<i>some memory loss,</i>

01:39.182 --> 01:40.183
<i>difficulty speaking.</i>

01:44.021 --> 01:45.856
<i>Body movement detected.</i>

01:46.648 --> 01:47.858
<i>For your own safety,</i>

01:47.941 --> 01:50.444
<i>please return to the medical platform.</i>

01:58.285 --> 01:59.745
<i>Where am I?</i>

01:59.828 --> 02:02.998
<i>Please remember</i>
<i>muscle function has not yet been restored.</i>

02:18.680 --> 02:19.681
<i>People!</i>

02:20.557 --> 02:21.725
<i>Hello?</i>

03:07.479 --> 03:09.064
<i>Where am I?</i>

03:50.063 --> 03:51.523
<i>Red leather, yellow leather.</i>

03:56.903 --> 04:00.031
<i>Why is there an atomic resolution</i>
<i>scanning electron microscope?</i>

04:01.032 --> 04:02.075
<i>Why do I know that?</i>

04:03.410 --> 04:04.661
Am I smart?

04:40.989 --> 04:42.157
Hello?

04:43.158 --> 04:44.326
<i>Please record video diary.</i>

04:44.409 --> 04:45.619
No.

04:45.702 --> 04:48.747
Can I speak to the person in charge?

04:50.040 --> 04:51.208
The, uh, captain.

04:51.291 --> 04:54.002
<i>Captain YÃ¡o, Li-Jie. Deceased.</i>

04:54.085 --> 04:57.422
Yeah, where, um, are the living people?

04:58.465 --> 05:00.175
<i>Dr. Ryland Grace.</i>

05:00.801 --> 05:02.093
<i>End of manifest.</i>

05:05.263 --> 05:09.976
I can't be the only person here. Right?

05:10.060 --> 05:11.186
<i>Pilot detected.</i>

05:11.269 --> 05:13.647
No! No. No-No. Not the pilot.

05:13.730 --> 05:15.232
Uh...

05:15.315 --> 05:16.817
Call, uh, Houston.

05:17.692 --> 05:18.693
<i>Unknown command.</i>

05:18.777 --> 05:21.738
Operation Go Home in effect.

05:21.822 --> 05:23.240
<i>Invalid operation.</i>

05:24.866 --> 05:27.327
That's the sun! There it is.

05:27.953 --> 05:30.205
So, what are we, like, Neptune-ish?

05:31.998 --> 05:34.918
Let's crank up the radio and call Earth.

05:35.001 --> 05:37.045
<i>Current transmission time to Earth</i>

05:37.128 --> 05:42.008
<i>is 11 years, 10 months,</i>
<i>14 days and 6 hours.</i>

05:42.092 --> 05:44.803
No. Nope. You're wrong.

05:44.886 --> 05:46.263
I need a map.

05:48.306 --> 05:49.391
Oh.

05:49.933 --> 05:50.934
Uh...

05:51.017 --> 05:52.811
That's so far.

05:52.894 --> 05:55.689
That's really far. Uh...

06:06.324 --> 06:08.034
That's not our sun.

06:11.788 --> 06:13.707
That's not our sun.

06:27.304 --> 06:28.889
Where is the fuel?

06:35.270 --> 06:36.271
Wait.

07:10.430 --> 07:13.099
<i>We are just minutes away</i>
<i>from the fly-by</i>

07:13.183 --> 07:15.268
<i>through the Petrova line near Venus.</i>

07:15.352 --> 07:18.021
<i>So, what can you tell us</i>
<i>about the ArcLight probe?</i>

07:18.688 --> 07:21.066
<i>What do we expect to find?</i>

07:22.734 --> 07:25.528
<i>The image we see will be coming</i>
<i>from the onboard microscope.</i>

07:29.866 --> 07:31.576
Um... Oh, my God.

07:36.539 --> 07:38.708
<i>Sorry. I have to go now.</i>

07:47.550 --> 07:53.056
Sound waves are physical.

07:53.139 --> 07:57.435
And at different frequencies,

07:57.519 --> 08:00.480
they make different pattern--

08:00.563 --> 08:03.191
No, pattern-- They make different patt--

08:03.274 --> 08:05.485
Olivia, can you help me out? There we go!

08:07.362 --> 08:08.363
Whoa!

08:09.280 --> 08:10.281
Yeah.

08:10.365 --> 08:12.283
Are they really eating the sun?

08:12.367 --> 08:14.411
The space dots?

08:14.494 --> 08:16.538
That is a great question, uh, Rekha.

08:16.621 --> 08:19.791
And I'd be curious
what your parents think about that.

08:19.874 --> 08:21.835
I'm not sure it should come from me.

08:21.918 --> 08:24.379
Hey! Who wants to play a game of...

08:24.462 --> 08:26.589
the bean bag is lava!

08:26.673 --> 08:29.384
Lava! Lava! Lava!

08:29.467 --> 08:31.845
What is the speed of light, Olivia?

08:31.928 --> 08:32.929
Uh...

08:33.013 --> 08:34.431
It's melting your hands, Olivia!

08:34.514 --> 08:37.017
186,000 miles per second!

08:37.100 --> 08:39.019
Oh! Give her a round of applause.

08:39.102 --> 08:41.271
That is awesome.

08:41.354 --> 08:43.648
What is the Petrova line?

08:44.649 --> 08:45.650
Pass!

08:45.734 --> 08:47.068
You can't pass!

08:47.152 --> 08:48.319
I just did.

08:48.403 --> 08:50.530
Lava! Lava! Lava!

08:50.613 --> 08:52.115
Come on, guys.

08:52.198 --> 08:53.616
Two years ago...

08:54.868 --> 08:58.705
a radio telescope enthusiast
named Irina Petrova,

08:59.664 --> 09:01.124
she noticed that there was a streak

09:01.207 --> 09:04.377
of infrared light from the sun to Venus.

09:04.961 --> 09:07.505
And that is now known as the Petrova line.

09:07.589 --> 09:08.590
That's it.

09:08.673 --> 09:10.091
That's the dots?

09:10.175 --> 09:11.176
They think so.

09:11.259 --> 09:12.677
What dots?

09:16.347 --> 09:18.349
These dots.

09:18.433 --> 09:20.560
They sent a probe up to the Petrova line,

09:20.643 --> 09:22.562
and this is what they found.

09:23.897 --> 09:25.607
Are they eating the sun?

09:27.567 --> 09:29.194
They seem to be...

09:29.277 --> 09:33.656
dimming the sun a tiny,
teeny-weeny, little bit.

09:34.365 --> 09:35.617
So, no big whoop.

09:35.700 --> 09:36.868
It's a small whoop.

09:37.494 --> 09:38.703
It's a small-to-medium whoop.

09:39.704 --> 09:43.124
Over the next 30 years, the Earth could...

09:43.208 --> 09:45.877
cool maybe 10...

09:46.920 --> 09:48.379
to 15 degrees.

09:49.130 --> 09:51.049
So it <i>is</i> a big whoop.

09:51.132 --> 09:53.468
I heard that all the crops will die,

09:53.551 --> 09:55.011
the economy will free-fall,

09:55.095 --> 09:56.679
and half the Earth will die of starvation.

09:57.889 --> 10:00.100
-Well...
-We're all gonna die!

10:00.183 --> 10:01.518
Yeah, it's true.

10:01.601 --> 10:02.936
You're forgetting something.

10:03.019 --> 10:06.147
Guys, guys!
That's <i>if</i> that were to happen. Right?

10:06.231 --> 10:08.358
They're gonna figure this out.

10:08.441 --> 10:13.738
Right now, the best minds
in the entire world are on it.

10:39.013 --> 10:40.932
<i>Good morning, Dr. Grace.</i>

10:42.267 --> 10:43.685
I'm up!

10:43.685 --> 11:13.685
<b>Subtitle | www.movieddl.me</b>

12:23.201 --> 12:24.285
Knock, knock.

12:26.120 --> 12:27.163
Who's there?

12:27.247 --> 12:28.414
Not good at jokes.

12:28.498 --> 12:29.707
Not good at jokes who?

12:33.878 --> 12:35.338
Dr. Grace?

12:37.215 --> 12:38.216
Maybe.

12:38.299 --> 12:40.134
Eva Stratt. I'm with
the Petrova Task Force.

12:40.218 --> 12:41.219
I need your help.

12:41.803 --> 12:42.762
Me.

12:42.845 --> 12:44.222
Did you write this?

12:47.183 --> 12:48.351
Oh.

12:48.434 --> 12:50.061
I'm interested in this section here.

12:50.144 --> 12:51.813
-Uh...
-Page 31.

12:51.896 --> 12:53.606
"The Goldilocks Zone Is For Idiots:

12:53.690 --> 12:55.900
Why Everyone Is Wrong About Life."

12:56.609 --> 12:57.819
That was a long time ago.

12:57.902 --> 12:59.696
Do you stand by what you wrote?

12:59.779 --> 13:01.864
I was fired for standing by what I wrote.

13:01.948 --> 13:03.199
You were fired

13:03.283 --> 13:05.535
for calling the leading
scholar in your field

13:05.618 --> 13:08.871
"A staggering waste of carbon"
at the UNESCO conference in Denmark.

13:08.955 --> 13:11.499
You heard about that, huh?

13:11.582 --> 13:13.835
Look, I don't know what this is,

13:13.918 --> 13:16.879
but, uh, I think I'm not s--

13:16.963 --> 13:18.172
That's lava.

13:18.256 --> 13:19.632
- It's not.
- Okay.

13:19.716 --> 13:22.302
No one in your field
wants anything to do with you

13:22.385 --> 13:25.013
because you refused to back down
from a very unpopular view,

13:25.096 --> 13:26.973
and I can give you a chance
to prove them all wrong.

13:27.056 --> 13:28.975
Uh... Kevin, no running.

13:29.058 --> 13:30.226
I'm not.

13:30.310 --> 13:33.771
Well, joke's on them because
I don't even care. So...

13:35.064 --> 13:38.234
I think you do care.
You're running away because you're scared.

13:38.318 --> 13:39.610
No, I'm not.

13:41.362 --> 13:44.532
Do you still believe
water is unnecessary for life to evolve?

13:44.615 --> 13:45.616
Uh...

13:46.242 --> 13:49.996
Look, there is nothing magical
about hydrogen and oxygen.

13:50.079 --> 13:52.415
Water is required for life on Earth, sure.

13:52.498 --> 13:54.000
But a completely different planet

13:54.083 --> 13:55.335
might have completely
different conditions.

13:55.418 --> 13:57.295
I don't know why that makes me such a nut.

13:57.378 --> 13:58.963
I need you to come with us.

14:00.548 --> 14:01.549
Sorry, I didn't catch your name.

14:01.632 --> 14:02.884
Carl.

14:02.967 --> 14:04.052
Carl. Hi.

14:04.135 --> 14:06.512
Dr. Grace, Petrova line samples
splashed down last night.

14:06.596 --> 14:09.432
I want you to tell me
what they are, how they work.

14:09.515 --> 14:12.268
I am a teacher at Grover Cleveland Middle.

14:12.352 --> 14:14.187
And you have a doctorate
in molecular biology.

14:14.270 --> 14:18.024
And I ride a bike to work,
and it's not for exercise.

14:18.107 --> 14:20.318
So I'm sure there are thousands
of other people who are better--

14:20.401 --> 14:22.362
It survives on the surface of the sun.

14:22.445 --> 14:24.572
Does that sound like
a water-based life-form to you?

14:31.537 --> 14:32.955
The sun's really dying, isn't it?

14:33.039 --> 14:34.457
Yeah.

14:38.878 --> 14:42.048
Is all of this... necessary?

14:43.508 --> 14:44.801
Yes.

14:44.884 --> 14:47.512
<i>Please analyze the Petrova sample.</i>

14:47.595 --> 14:48.596
Um...

14:48.679 --> 14:49.680
<i>Just one thing.</i>

14:49.764 --> 14:51.474
The entire room is filled with argon.

14:51.557 --> 14:52.975
Just try not to rip your suit.

14:54.310 --> 14:55.436
Uh...

14:57.814 --> 15:00.108
Am I expendable? Is that why you want me?

15:00.191 --> 15:02.151
That's not the only reason.

15:02.235 --> 15:04.278
It's almost like you don't care if I die.

15:08.866 --> 15:10.910
Hold on. You have to talk about it?

15:11.869 --> 15:16.165
The consensus here is that
it would be preferable if you did not die.

15:17.500 --> 15:18.626
Thanks, guys.

15:25.967 --> 15:27.009
Oh.

15:32.557 --> 15:34.642
Welcome to Earth.

15:49.490 --> 15:50.867
Are they alive?

15:51.534 --> 15:52.785
They're moving.

15:52.869 --> 15:54.328
So they're alive.

15:54.412 --> 15:58.082
Yes, they're moving,
but that could be for many reasons.

15:58.166 --> 15:59.584
What are they made of?

15:59.667 --> 16:01.919
It's gonna take a long ti--
It took like 200 years

16:02.003 --> 16:03.880
to figure out how bacteria works, so...

16:03.963 --> 16:05.631
Please do it faster.

16:08.092 --> 16:10.720
I'm just going through
the whole light spectrum here.

16:11.762 --> 16:12.889
Wow!

16:14.307 --> 16:15.975
You're not gonna believe this.

16:19.020 --> 16:20.021
Nothing happened.

16:25.443 --> 16:27.778
I can't see into these buggers.

16:29.530 --> 16:30.781
Amazing.

16:30.865 --> 16:34.827
X-rays, microwaves,
gamma rays, visible light.

16:34.911 --> 16:36.329
Nothing!

16:38.080 --> 16:40.958
Ooh, ooh, ooh, I've been
dying to do this all day!

16:57.850 --> 16:58.893
It's a cell!

16:58.976 --> 17:01.896
It's a cell. Wake up! It's a cell!

17:01.979 --> 17:05.066
It's a tiny alien cell. You guys!

17:05.149 --> 17:06.609
Applause.

17:06.692 --> 17:09.487
Guys! This is first contact!

17:10.238 --> 17:12.865
With life outside of the--

17:13.616 --> 17:14.784
Uh-oh.

17:15.451 --> 17:16.827
Oh, it died.

17:17.662 --> 17:18.871
What?

17:19.622 --> 17:21.040
This is great news.

17:21.123 --> 17:23.042
Now we can find out what they're made of.

17:25.795 --> 17:27.338
You guys fired up?

17:30.967 --> 17:32.134
Yes!

17:32.718 --> 17:33.803
Right?

17:34.762 --> 17:36.138
Carbon.

17:36.764 --> 17:38.266
Oxygen.

17:40.643 --> 17:42.353
Hydrogen.

17:42.436 --> 17:43.688
Oh.

17:58.202 --> 17:59.870
It's made...

18:01.956 --> 18:03.833
... almost entirely...

18:06.210 --> 18:07.670
of water.

18:17.054 --> 18:18.055
All right.

18:21.142 --> 18:23.227
Sorry if I overreacted in there.

18:23.978 --> 18:28.441
Just, uh, realizing I was wrong
about the only original idea I ever had.

18:28.524 --> 18:30.318
Yeah. What else did you learn?

18:30.401 --> 18:32.486
They give off infrared light
when they move.

18:32.570 --> 18:33.571
Mm-hmm.

18:33.654 --> 18:34.655
Like, so much.

18:34.739 --> 18:37.199
I don't know how
they store all that energy.

18:37.283 --> 18:41.954
But, uh, their wavelength is
exactly the Petrova frequency.

18:42.038 --> 18:43.873
The light is how they move.

18:43.956 --> 18:47.126
Yeah, they consume the sun's energy,
and then they expel it for propulsion.

18:47.668 --> 18:49.337
They toot to scoot basically.

18:51.380 --> 18:53.257
Why did it go to Venus?

18:55.134 --> 18:56.469
I don't know.

18:57.762 --> 18:59.472
Okay. We'll tell you.

18:59.555 --> 19:00.806
What?

19:00.890 --> 19:03.559
I have 347 other biologists
in 21 countries

19:03.643 --> 19:05.061
mobilizing as we speak.

19:05.144 --> 19:07.855
So thank you for your help.
I'm glad you didn't die.

19:07.938 --> 19:09.231
Let's pack this up.

19:09.982 --> 19:10.983
Pack up!

19:13.653 --> 19:15.363
That's it?

19:15.446 --> 19:17.198
-That's it, Carl?
-Talk to her.

19:17.281 --> 19:18.658
You're taking all the stuff?

19:18.741 --> 19:19.909
At the school you said

19:19.992 --> 19:22.161
there were thousands of people
more qualified than you, so...

19:22.244 --> 19:24.664
-I was being modest.
-I don't need modest.

19:24.747 --> 19:27.708
I need people who think they're right
when everyone else thinks they're wrong.

19:27.792 --> 19:29.335
I need people who piss other people off.

19:29.418 --> 19:30.461
That's me!

19:30.544 --> 19:32.338
Do you want to be on a project?

19:32.421 --> 19:34.548
If you...

19:34.632 --> 19:37.593
If you think I can help, then...

19:37.677 --> 19:39.637
Just answer the question.

19:39.720 --> 19:41.597
I wanna help the world.

19:41.681 --> 19:43.140
I left you three dots.

19:43.224 --> 19:44.642
Just three, huh?

19:44.725 --> 19:46.060
Plus the one you killed.

19:47.103 --> 19:48.604
I would take the three.

19:51.023 --> 19:52.525
-Yes?
-I'll take the dots.

19:52.608 --> 19:54.110
Get to work, Dr. Grace.

19:55.820 --> 19:58.155
The world is counting on you.

21:05.931 --> 21:07.224
<i>Hey, Carl?</i>

21:08.476 --> 21:09.727
This is Carl.

21:09.810 --> 21:13.939
Imagine for a second
that you're an interstellar microorganism.

21:14.023 --> 21:15.733
I'm not doing that.

21:17.568 --> 21:20.404
Why would you
leave the sun and go to Venus?

21:20.488 --> 21:23.115
Why not stop at Mercury? It's closer.

21:24.658 --> 21:26.577
Maybe fresh air?

21:26.660 --> 21:28.496
Venus's atmosphere is mostly CO2.

21:28.579 --> 21:30.122
Maybe that's fresh air to them.

21:30.206 --> 21:32.124
You don't know what
these little space dots like.

21:37.421 --> 21:38.547
<i>Hey, Carl?</i>

21:38.631 --> 21:40.466
Do we have an expense account?

21:40.549 --> 21:42.218
We don't.

21:46.555 --> 21:47.598
But I do.

22:19.171 --> 22:20.714
All right, I got something for you.

22:31.517 --> 22:32.518
Thank you.

22:32.601 --> 22:34.603
-Do you have a coupon?
-We don't need a coupon.

22:34.687 --> 22:36.605
-We're the government.
-Which government?

22:36.689 --> 22:37.982
All of 'em.

22:39.900 --> 22:41.443
So this box is Venus.

22:41.527 --> 22:43.279
The box is Venus.

22:43.362 --> 22:45.322
It's set to emit the same
infrared light frequency

22:45.406 --> 22:47.241
as Venus's CO2 atmosphere.

22:47.324 --> 22:49.493
So if the dots move
when you turn the lights on,

22:49.577 --> 22:52.162
that means they're looking for CO2.

22:52.246 --> 22:54.665
Thus proving the Carl Hypothesis.

22:54.748 --> 22:55.749
Sick.

22:58.252 --> 22:59.670
Sorry.

22:59.753 --> 23:00.880
-All right.
-All right.

23:00.963 --> 23:02.006
You ready?

23:05.926 --> 23:06.886
Uh-oh.

23:07.845 --> 23:09.305
Why you saying "Uh-oh"?

23:09.388 --> 23:11.348
The Astrophage
moved off the slide.

23:11.432 --> 23:12.641
Huh?

23:12.725 --> 23:14.018
We lost them.

23:14.101 --> 23:16.312
What do you mean,
we lost them? Go get them.

23:16.854 --> 23:18.647
If I open the door in the light,

23:18.731 --> 23:20.649
they're gonna escape,
and then we'll lose them forever.

23:20.733 --> 23:21.942
At least now they're in the box.

23:22.026 --> 23:23.319
So we'll just turn the lights off.

23:23.402 --> 23:25.487
But there'll still be light.
It's gotta be total darkness.

23:25.571 --> 23:27.823
Okay, so just put this box in another box.

23:35.956 --> 23:37.041
Nice.

23:49.845 --> 23:52.056
Go ahead and close it. Over.

23:52.765 --> 23:54.224
I can hear you.

23:59.813 --> 24:01.774
Entering phase two.

24:07.404 --> 24:08.697
I'm in.

24:13.911 --> 24:15.746
<i>Please report. Over.</i>

24:18.874 --> 24:20.334
<i>There's one.</i>

24:21.961 --> 24:22.962
<i>There's two!</i>

24:23.045 --> 24:25.089
<i>Come on! Get the third one.</i>

24:27.299 --> 24:28.300
<i>Got sample three.</i>

24:28.384 --> 24:31.011
<i>The Three Musketeers. The Three Stooges.</i>

24:34.848 --> 24:36.058
<i>Uh-oh.</i>

24:36.141 --> 24:37.768
<i>What? What?</i>

24:38.978 --> 24:40.729
<i>Are you sitting down?</i>

24:40.813 --> 24:42.564
No, I'm standing like a grown man.

24:42.648 --> 24:44.650
<i>'Cause there's a fourth.</i>

24:45.567 --> 24:46.568
What?

24:48.737 --> 24:50.948
<i>We're fathers, Carl.</i>

24:53.367 --> 24:55.369
Now I'm sitting down.

24:58.288 --> 24:59.289
<i>Stratt.</i>

24:59.373 --> 25:01.208
Carl and I made a baby.

25:02.418 --> 25:03.752
<i>What?</i>

25:03.836 --> 25:05.713
We figured out how Astrophage breeds.

25:07.006 --> 25:08.257
<i>Don't move.</i>

25:10.050 --> 25:10.884
Wheels up.

25:14.430 --> 25:16.765
You know, Carl, when Stratt said
she was sending a jet,

25:16.849 --> 25:18.392
I thought you meant
like a PJ or something.

25:18.475 --> 25:21.145
-I know.
-Take this.

25:22.646 --> 25:24.106
Been a long time since I've taken a pill

25:24.189 --> 25:25.733
from a stranger
without knowing what it was.

25:28.027 --> 25:30.070
Stop! Stop!

25:40.539 --> 25:42.374
Dr. Grace, how was your flight?

25:48.005 --> 25:49.631
-Where are we?
-The ocean.

25:49.715 --> 25:52.676
The Astrophage reproduction.
Can you do it at scale?

25:52.760 --> 25:56.597
Oh, in theory, you just get
a bunch of elbow-shaped pipe,

25:56.680 --> 25:58.057
put sunlight in one end--

25:58.140 --> 25:59.308
Excuse me for a second.

26:00.726 --> 26:02.227
-Write it down.
-Yes, ma'am.

26:03.604 --> 26:06.690
... and IR light and CO2
in the other. Repeat.

26:06.774 --> 26:07.900
Anything else you need for--

26:07.983 --> 26:08.984
Just some water.

26:09.068 --> 26:10.861
He gave me a pill
and it's still in my throat.

26:10.944 --> 26:12.279
Two venti coffees.

26:12.362 --> 26:13.781
Thank you so much. Water for him.

26:13.864 --> 26:14.948
Or coffee. That's...

26:15.032 --> 26:16.033
I need both.

26:16.116 --> 26:17.117
Or not.

26:17.201 --> 26:20.913
Okay, so this is kind of a tough room,
so don't take it personally.

26:20.996 --> 26:22.122
I don't need anything fancy.

26:22.206 --> 26:24.750
As long as there's a bed
and a shower I'm good.

26:29.755 --> 26:31.632
- I can't do this.
- Come on. Yes.

26:31.715 --> 26:33.509
- I can't. I can't.
- You're doing great.

26:33.592 --> 26:37.179
Everyone, this is Dr. Ryland Grace
from the United States.

26:37.262 --> 26:39.473
Please go here. Thank you.

26:39.556 --> 26:42.392
And he figured out
how to breed Astrophage.

26:47.940 --> 26:48.941
How did you do it?

26:49.024 --> 26:50.943
How long does the process take?

26:51.026 --> 26:53.445
Does it reproduce by mitosis or meiosis?

26:53.529 --> 26:55.781
<i>What is the incubation period?</i>

26:57.074 --> 26:58.075
Um...

27:00.160 --> 27:02.621
Carl and I made a mini Venus
out of a plywood box,

27:03.247 --> 27:05.624
and as soon as the samples recognized

27:05.707 --> 27:08.710
the, uh, spectral signature
of CO2, they were...

27:11.046 --> 27:12.840
They were like, "Whoomp! There it is!"

27:12.923 --> 27:14.007
Okay.

27:14.091 --> 27:15.592
You can sit down.

27:15.676 --> 27:16.677
Who's Carl?

27:16.760 --> 27:18.470
While your experiment
was crude and clumsy,

27:18.554 --> 27:21.223
our scientists
have replicated his results.

27:24.059 --> 27:25.936
I'm sorry, how did you see--

27:26.019 --> 27:29.148
We estimate an eight-day doubling time
under optimal conditions.

27:29.231 --> 27:31.441
-Dr. Grace is right.
-Oh.

27:31.525 --> 27:32.901
It is the CO2.

27:32.985 --> 27:34.528
That's why they're going to Venus.

27:34.611 --> 27:35.612
Please say it for everyone.

27:35.696 --> 27:37.447
That's why they're going to Venus.

27:37.531 --> 27:40.534
So that's why they're going to Venus,
is what Dr. Grace wants to say.

27:40.617 --> 27:42.411
So that's why they're going to Venus,
is what Dr. Grace wants to say.

27:42.494 --> 27:43.954
They're going to breed.

27:44.037 --> 27:45.873
Whoomp! There it is.

27:47.499 --> 27:49.293
I really like him.

27:49.376 --> 27:51.837
How long to make two million kilogram?

27:51.920 --> 27:53.380
Two million?

27:55.299 --> 27:56.466
Two million is--

27:56.550 --> 27:57.593
Why...

27:59.428 --> 28:01.763
on earth would you need
that much Astrophage?

28:01.847 --> 28:03.932
Nobody told him?

28:04.600 --> 28:05.767
He has no clearance.

28:05.851 --> 28:07.644
Please stand up, Dr. Grace.

28:08.437 --> 28:09.521
Stand up.

28:10.606 --> 28:12.357
I hereby grant you top-secret clearance

28:12.441 --> 28:14.943
to all information pertaining
to Project Hail Mary.

28:16.236 --> 28:17.779
What's Project Hail Mary?

28:19.698 --> 28:20.699
All right.

28:24.119 --> 28:26.580
The sun is not the only star dying.

28:26.663 --> 28:28.957
There is a clear pattern of infection.

28:29.041 --> 28:32.920
Every star was infected
by its neighbor except one.

28:33.003 --> 28:34.046
Tau Ceti?

28:34.129 --> 28:36.173
That's right. 11.9 light years away.

28:36.256 --> 28:39.927
It is uninfected despite being well within
the cluster of infected stars.

28:40.010 --> 28:41.011
Why?

28:41.094 --> 28:42.095
Why? Tell him.

28:42.179 --> 28:43.847
We don't know!

28:43.931 --> 28:47.893
Which is why we decided to build a ship
to go there and find out.

28:47.976 --> 28:49.478
It's 11.9 light years away.

28:49.561 --> 28:51.855
You can't just build an interstellar ship.

28:51.939 --> 28:53.232
Oh, yeah, we can.

28:53.315 --> 28:54.858
The ship is not really the problem.

28:54.942 --> 28:58.320
The energy required to power
the ship is the problem.

28:58.403 --> 28:59.488
It was the problem.

29:02.032 --> 29:03.659
The Astrophage is the fuel.

29:03.742 --> 29:05.494
Provided we can make enough of it.

29:05.577 --> 29:08.163
And for that, we need you, my friend.

29:10.540 --> 29:11.667
Me?

29:11.750 --> 29:14.753
Ooh. Those little guys
store a lot of energy.

29:15.379 --> 29:19.132
One mistake and that much Astrophage
could vaporize California.

29:19.216 --> 29:21.385
That's true, which is why
you now live on a boat

29:21.468 --> 29:23.178
in the middle of the ocean in case...

29:24.763 --> 29:26.848
-I live on a boat?
-You do.

29:26.932 --> 29:29.434
So you wanna build
a near-light speed spaceship,

29:29.518 --> 29:31.770
have it travel further
than any human-made object

29:31.853 --> 29:32.938
has ever traveled,

29:33.021 --> 29:36.858
and visit a star just to "see what's up"?

29:36.942 --> 29:38.068
Yeah.

29:38.151 --> 29:39.403
And then what?

29:39.486 --> 29:41.571
There won't be
enough fuel for a round-trip

29:41.655 --> 29:44.783
so they send their findings
back to Earth on probes.

29:47.202 --> 29:49.871
And the astronauts...

29:53.125 --> 29:54.167
die in space?

29:54.751 --> 29:55.961
Yes.

29:58.088 --> 29:59.673
Space.

30:03.135 --> 30:05.804
Any other plans you've thought of?

30:05.887 --> 30:08.682
Anything else you're kicking around?

30:08.765 --> 30:12.686
There are infinite possibilities
for this to go wrong.

30:12.769 --> 30:13.979
It almost certainly won't work.

30:14.062 --> 30:16.606
It's what you Americans
would call a long shot.

30:17.357 --> 30:18.692
Hail Mary.

30:19.735 --> 30:20.569
I get it.

30:20.652 --> 30:24.156
The alternative is to just do nothing

30:24.990 --> 30:27.117
and to starve and to kill each other

30:27.200 --> 30:29.536
and watch everything
on this planet go extinct,

30:29.619 --> 30:30.871
including us.

30:31.538 --> 30:32.581
So...

30:53.060 --> 30:55.562
That's a long time to be in space.

30:55.645 --> 30:57.272
They'll be in comas
for most of it.

30:57.856 --> 30:59.566
But is that even safe?

30:59.649 --> 31:01.610
Nothing about this is safe.

31:16.541 --> 31:17.834
We only need three.

31:18.835 --> 31:19.961
A pilot.

31:20.045 --> 31:22.047
An engineer.

31:22.130 --> 31:24.299
And a scientist.

31:29.137 --> 31:30.680
Commander YÃ¡o.

31:33.058 --> 31:35.560
I must have known you,
but I just don't remember.

31:36.520 --> 31:39.064
You make a funny face

31:39.147 --> 31:42.776
in literally every picture.

31:43.819 --> 31:46.696
You must have been very smart

31:46.780 --> 31:47.781
and strong

31:49.658 --> 31:50.992
and brave.

31:55.122 --> 31:56.123
Ilyukhina.

31:57.666 --> 32:00.085
First, I owe you three bags of vodka.

32:01.294 --> 32:03.463
You seemed to have had a lot of friends.

32:04.172 --> 32:05.382
This picture of you,

32:05.465 --> 32:08.885
what looks like sneaking into the Kremlin...

32:10.679 --> 32:11.680
is legendary.

32:14.599 --> 32:16.393
I wish you were still here.

32:17.853 --> 32:20.021
I wish I wasn't alone.

32:20.105 --> 32:21.857
I wish I was...

32:22.983 --> 32:25.193
doing a better job, I just, uh...

32:30.115 --> 32:31.950
You were both very loved.

32:33.702 --> 32:35.036
You deserve much more than this.

32:37.914 --> 32:41.084
I'll do my best
to make sure that you don't...

32:43.795 --> 32:45.755
... you know, that you didn't--

32:57.726 --> 32:59.311
I'll do my best.

34:04.876 --> 34:07.045
<i>Approaching Tau Ceti orbit.</i>

34:07.128 --> 34:08.421
<i>Prepare for engine cutoff.</i>

34:10.090 --> 34:11.591
-Fourteen, thirteen...
-What?

34:11.675 --> 34:13.051
Why engine cutoff?

34:13.134 --> 34:15.804
Seems like something we should talk about.

34:15.887 --> 34:17.556
<i>Pilot detected.</i>

34:17.639 --> 34:19.766
What? No. I'm not a pilot!

34:19.849 --> 34:21.268
<i>Four, three...</i>

34:21.351 --> 34:23.270
What happens at zero? I mean--

34:31.194 --> 34:33.530
<i>You are now orbiting Tau Ceti.</i>

34:36.533 --> 34:40.245
What the fudging fudger?

34:43.039 --> 34:45.292
<i>Petrovascope operational.</i>

34:45.375 --> 34:47.377
<i>Petrovascope operational.</i>

35:08.607 --> 35:09.691
It's Tau Ceti.

35:20.702 --> 35:23.121
You have a Petrova line.

35:23.204 --> 35:25.040
But you're not dimming.

35:25.123 --> 35:27.000
Why?

35:37.052 --> 35:38.428
What is that?

35:56.071 --> 35:57.822
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

35:58.782 --> 36:00.200
What's a Blip-A?

36:01.117 --> 36:03.328
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

37:08.309 --> 37:11.646
No, no. No, no, no.
Let's go. Let's go, Mary.

37:11.730 --> 37:13.273
No-No-No-No!

37:13.356 --> 37:14.524
Pilot detected!

37:14.607 --> 37:15.942
<i>Please engage restraint.</i>

37:16.025 --> 37:17.026
Pilot detected!

37:17.110 --> 37:19.237
<i>-Pilot detected.</i>
<i>-We're getting outta here.</i>

37:19.320 --> 37:20.363
<i>Engage spin drive.</i>

37:20.447 --> 37:23.283
-Engage the spin drive.
<i>-Incorrect.</i>

37:23.366 --> 37:24.701
<i>Welcome to the Hail Mary.</i>

37:24.784 --> 37:25.994
Is it this thing?

37:31.207 --> 37:33.710
<i>Erratic maneuver detected.</i>

37:35.795 --> 37:36.796
Yeah.

37:53.438 --> 37:55.064
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

37:57.317 --> 37:59.194
What do they want?

38:29.599 --> 38:30.600
What?

38:44.364 --> 38:45.865
<i>Blip-B detected.</i>

38:45.949 --> 38:47.116
What's a Bl--

38:47.200 --> 38:49.661
<i>Current distance</i>
from Hail Mary <i>is 800 meters.</i>

38:49.744 --> 38:51.788
It's sending me something.

38:54.082 --> 38:55.917
It could be a message.

38:58.670 --> 39:00.505
It could be a bomb.

39:01.214 --> 39:02.215
Is it a bomb?

39:03.967 --> 39:04.968
Shields up!

39:05.051 --> 39:06.636
<i>There are no shields</i>
<i>on board the</i> Hail Mary.

39:06.719 --> 39:07.929
Why not?

39:08.012 --> 39:09.389
<i>Thirty meters.</i>

39:09.889 --> 39:12.350
<i>Twenty meters. Ten.</i>

39:23.152 --> 39:26.364
Well, I guess there's egg
on both our faces, huh, Mary?

39:26.447 --> 39:28.157
<i>Blip-C detected.</i>

39:28.241 --> 39:29.701
What's that?

39:32.996 --> 39:35.540
Why is it moving so much
slower than the last one?

39:39.919 --> 39:41.254
They think I'm dumb.

39:46.092 --> 39:48.678
If we were gonna catch it,
what would we do?

39:48.761 --> 39:51.389
<i>Would you like to go</i>
<i>on a space walk, Dr. Grace?</i>

39:57.395 --> 39:58.938
<i>To begin your space walk,</i>

39:59.022 --> 40:02.442
<i>put on your EVA suit</i>
<i>and proceed to the air lock.</i>

40:12.869 --> 40:14.329
Yeah.

40:14.412 --> 40:15.872
Yes!

40:51.282 --> 40:52.450
No.

40:53.284 --> 40:54.911
That's just crazy.

42:18.661 --> 42:21.873
Righty tighty, lefty loosey.

42:26.502 --> 42:29.547
Xenon's a gas.
I think this thing's broken.

42:37.013 --> 42:38.723
We need equipment

42:38.806 --> 42:42.018
to analyze the Astrophage
at Tau Ceti. Correct?

42:42.101 --> 42:44.062
-Dr. Grace.
-Sorry. Hi.

42:44.145 --> 42:46.314
Most of this equipment
won't work in zero gravity.

42:46.397 --> 42:48.274
And we have a team
to develop and build

42:48.357 --> 42:49.984
zero gravity versions of this equipment.

42:50.068 --> 42:52.528
We would need years for that,
and we have months.

42:52.612 --> 42:53.654
What's the alternative?

42:53.738 --> 42:55.156
We make gravity.

42:55.239 --> 42:56.574
Centrifuge?

42:56.657 --> 42:58.159
Centrifuge.

42:58.242 --> 43:00.703
They used them to make butter
during the Civil War,

43:00.787 --> 43:02.080
actually.

43:02.163 --> 43:04.540
It's a fun fact.

43:58.302 --> 44:00.179
<i>Lab operational.</i>

44:13.067 --> 44:14.110
Still Xenon!

44:14.986 --> 44:17.864
Up is down! Left is right!
Xenon's a solid!

44:17.947 --> 44:19.198
Aliens are real!

44:19.282 --> 44:22.201
I'm wrong about everything
and everything's wrong.

44:26.956 --> 44:28.958
Lefty tighty, righty loosey.

44:30.459 --> 44:31.502
I mean--

44:32.211 --> 44:33.796
Oh, no!

44:33.880 --> 44:35.256
<i>Foreign presence detected.</i>

45:39.111 --> 45:41.239
You're a long way from home.

45:58.047 --> 46:00.091
<i>I'm a long way from home too.</i>

46:19.402 --> 46:20.903
<i>Blip-D detected.</i>

50:33.364 --> 50:34.865
Is that me?

50:47.378 --> 50:48.587
My ship.

50:58.389 --> 50:59.848
I don't understand.

51:04.186 --> 51:05.396
Oh.

51:06.438 --> 51:08.357
You want me...

51:10.109 --> 51:11.860
to go back in my ship?

51:15.072 --> 51:16.198
But I just got here.

51:19.785 --> 51:21.412
Okay.

51:22.496 --> 51:23.706
Okay.

51:26.208 --> 51:28.210
I'll talk to you later.

51:30.004 --> 51:31.005
Bye.

52:08.917 --> 52:10.919
What are they doing, Mary?

52:40.824 --> 52:44.286
<i>It is not advised to modify</i>
<i>the centrifugal settings of the ship.</i>

52:44.370 --> 52:45.579
Thank you, Mary.

53:57.651 --> 53:58.694
What?

54:18.505 --> 54:19.631
Oh, my.

54:21.467 --> 54:22.509
This is new.

54:35.564 --> 54:36.857
Anybody home?

54:40.527 --> 54:42.613
I like what you've done with the gravity.

54:49.620 --> 54:50.996
I made you a ship.

54:55.292 --> 54:56.919
It's ramen.

54:59.046 --> 55:01.006
I only made one.

55:02.007 --> 55:05.427
I'm not sure how many of you there are.

55:55.602 --> 55:56.937
Hi.

56:24.214 --> 56:26.133
No-No-No-No-No! I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

56:27.342 --> 56:29.219
You scared me when you went:

57:34.534 --> 57:35.911
Wow.

57:51.009 --> 57:54.179
Can't seem to see a face.

57:55.013 --> 57:56.390
Mysterious.

57:57.307 --> 57:58.934
Faces are overrated.

58:11.488 --> 58:13.699
You like tapping, huh?

58:32.843 --> 58:34.261
This is fun.

58:44.688 --> 58:46.481
You're pointing.

58:47.816 --> 58:50.110
You're not tapping. Sorry.

58:56.992 --> 58:58.785
Another present.

59:15.677 --> 59:17.721
I don't have one of these.

59:17.804 --> 59:20.557
I love it! Thank you.

59:23.727 --> 59:26.104
What, I put this on my head?

59:29.816 --> 59:31.068
Take off my head?

59:33.153 --> 59:34.613
Take my helmet off?

59:36.531 --> 59:37.908
No.

59:37.991 --> 59:39.034
It'll...

59:42.871 --> 59:44.081
That's a big ask.

59:45.290 --> 59:46.625
Oh.

59:49.836 --> 59:51.296
This has taken a turn.

59:51.379 --> 59:54.883
Thank you for
what I hope is not handcuffs.

59:55.884 --> 59:58.720
And I'm sorry
about the whole helmet thing.

59:58.804 --> 01:00:01.640
Unfortunately, I need oxygen
to breathe, so...

01:00:09.815 --> 01:00:11.399
Two rings of eight.

01:00:14.861 --> 01:00:16.404
O2.

01:00:18.824 --> 01:00:20.867
You little rascal.

01:00:23.453 --> 01:00:24.496
This is oxygen!

01:00:28.834 --> 01:00:31.878
I still don't think
I can do it, though. Sorry.

01:00:33.171 --> 01:00:34.673
It's just, uh...

01:00:34.756 --> 01:00:38.009
if I'm wrong, I'm really wrong.

01:01:36.693 --> 01:01:38.236
Okay.

01:02:01.927 --> 01:02:03.970
It's the Petrova line.

01:02:06.306 --> 01:02:07.891
We're here for the same reason.

01:02:07.974 --> 01:02:09.559
You have a Petrova line problem too.

01:02:09.643 --> 01:02:11.978
You need to solve it. I need to solve it.

01:02:12.062 --> 01:02:13.396
That's why you made me this.

01:02:16.942 --> 01:02:18.485
I'm gonna take that as a yes.

01:02:19.319 --> 01:02:20.987
If we're gonna save our planets,

01:02:21.071 --> 01:02:22.656
we have to learn how to communicate.

01:02:22.739 --> 01:02:25.242
You know, they say math
is the universal language.

01:02:25.325 --> 01:02:29.371
I thought maybe we find out
if that's true?

01:02:29.454 --> 01:02:31.039
You see the numbers?

01:02:33.208 --> 01:02:34.542
Oh, you want this?

01:02:36.336 --> 01:02:38.630
Whoa! Christmas Eve, that is...

01:02:38.713 --> 01:02:41.925
It's really hot in there. Is that ammonia?

01:02:42.008 --> 01:02:43.301
Anyway, that's...

01:02:44.427 --> 01:02:46.429
Look, see the numbers?

01:02:46.513 --> 01:02:47.722
No. They're on the other side.

01:02:47.806 --> 01:02:50.016
Do it again. If you do it again and you--

01:02:50.100 --> 01:02:51.101
Don't let it snap back.

01:02:51.184 --> 01:02:52.560
Don't do it. Don't do it.

01:02:52.644 --> 01:02:55.313
Hold it like that. And you look--

01:02:58.275 --> 01:03:00.694
Wow. Wow.

01:03:01.403 --> 01:03:02.654
How 'bout something else?

01:03:03.822 --> 01:03:05.031
Look at this.

01:03:05.824 --> 01:03:07.242
A clock.

01:03:07.325 --> 01:03:09.577
We both have time. Right?

01:03:09.661 --> 01:03:12.372
These are the hands,
and these are the numbers.

01:03:12.455 --> 01:03:14.749
One, two, three.
They point to the numbers.

01:03:16.001 --> 01:03:17.085
And if you'll notice,

01:03:17.168 --> 01:03:19.212
there's numbers there,
and there's also numbers here.

01:03:22.090 --> 01:03:23.091
Okay.

01:03:26.886 --> 01:03:28.263
It is dark.

01:03:37.731 --> 01:03:38.982
I got an idea.

01:03:39.065 --> 01:03:40.317
Wait.

01:03:41.443 --> 01:03:44.321
Don't... Yeah.

01:03:44.404 --> 01:03:46.031
Don't go anywhere. Stay.

01:03:46.698 --> 01:03:48.742
Oh. Oh yeah.

01:03:51.161 --> 01:03:52.162
I'm Grace.

01:03:54.581 --> 01:03:55.999
I'm gonna call you Rocky.

01:03:56.082 --> 01:03:59.294
You know, 'cause you look
like a giant rock. Oh!

01:04:00.045 --> 01:04:02.630
Um, I'm fine.

01:04:15.602 --> 01:04:17.812
Echolocation. Right?

01:04:17.896 --> 01:04:19.397
You need surfaces to see.

01:04:19.481 --> 01:04:20.690
Try this.

01:04:24.319 --> 01:04:27.030
You see the numbers?

01:04:29.199 --> 01:04:30.784
What is that?

01:04:30.867 --> 01:04:32.077
Is that good?

01:04:32.160 --> 01:04:33.870
Jazz hands means "good"?

01:04:35.413 --> 01:04:37.040
We do this.

